{ id:"mq-801", mod:"M8", topic:"Homeostasis", diff:3,
  q:"Drinking a large volume of water leads to a high volume of dilute urine because",
  options:["less ADH is secreted, so the collecting duct becomes less permeable to water","more ADH is secreted, which increases water reabsorption from the filtrate","aldosterone secretion rises, causing more sodium to be excreted in urine","the glomerular filtration rate falls, so less filtrate is formed each minute"], answer:0,
  why:"Osmoreceptors detect the fall in blood solute concentration and the posterior pituitary reduces ADH release. Fewer aquaporins are inserted into the collecting duct, so less water returns to the blood.",
  distractors:{1:"More ADH would produce a small volume of concentrated urine.",2:"Aldosterone controls sodium reabsorption and is not the primary response to dilution.",3:"Filtration rate is not the variable adjusted here; reabsorption is."},
  misconception:"ADH controls how much water comes back, not how much filtrate is made." },

{ id:"mq-802", mod:"M8", topic:"Homeostasis", diff:2,
  q:"Vasodilation of skin arterioles cools the body because it",
  options:["increases blood flow near the surface, so more heat is radiated away","reduces the metabolic rate of the tissues and so lowers heat production","causes sweat glands to secrete a much greater volume of dilute sweat","transfers warm blood to the deeper core vessels away from the surface"], answer:0,
  why:"Widening the arterioles supplying the superficial capillary network brings more warm blood close to the skin, increasing the temperature gradient with the environment and so the rate of radiation and convection.",
  distractors:{1:"Vasodilation changes heat loss, not the rate at which heat is produced.",2:"Sweating is a separate response controlled independently.",3:"That describes vasoconstriction, which conserves heat instead."},
  misconception:"" },

{ id:"mq-803", mod:"M8", topic:"Disorders", diff:3,
  q:"A person with myopia sees near objects clearly but distant objects blurred. The correcting lens must be",
  options:["concave, to diverge light so the focal point falls further back","convex, to converge light so the focal point moves further forward","cylindrical, to correct an unevenly curved corneal surface","tinted, to reduce the intensity of light entering the eye"], answer:0,
  why:"In myopia the eyeball is too long or the lens too powerful, so parallel light converges in front of the retina. A diverging lens shifts that focal point back onto the retina.",
  distractors:{1:"A converging lens corrects hyperopia, where the focus falls behind the retina.",2:"A cylindrical lens corrects astigmatism.",3:"Tinting alters brightness and does not change where light focuses."},
  misconception:"Focus in front of the retina needs divergence. Focus behind it needs convergence." },

{ id:"mq-804", mod:"M8", topic:"Epidemiology", diff:3,
  q:"A cohort study differs from a case-control study in that a cohort study",
  options:["follows exposed and unexposed groups forward to see who develops disease","selects people who already have the disease and looks back at their exposure","assigns participants to an exposure at random and compares the two groups","measures disease and exposure at a single point in time in one population"], answer:0,
  why:"Cohort studies are prospective: exposure is recorded first and outcomes accumulate over time, which is why they can measure incidence and establish the order of events.",
  distractors:{1:"That describes a case-control study, which is retrospective.",2:"Random assignment defines a randomised controlled trial.",3:"That describes a cross-sectional study."},
  misconception:"" },

{ id:"mq-805", mod:"M8", topic:"Disorders", diff:2,
  q:"Which best explains why a tumour is described as malignant rather than benign?",
  options:["Its cells invade surrounding tissue and can spread to distant sites","It grows more quickly than the normal tissue that surrounds it","It arises from a mutation in a gene controlling the rate of division","It reaches a larger final size than a benign tumour usually does"], answer:0,
  why:"Malignancy is defined by invasion and metastasis, not by growth rate or size. A benign tumour stays encapsulated in one place, which is why it is usually curable by removal.",
  distractors:{1:"Some benign tumours grow quickly and some malignant ones grow slowly.",2:"Both types arise from mutations affecting cell division.",3:"Benign tumours can become very large without being malignant."},
  misconception:"Spreading, not speed or size, is what makes a tumour dangerous." },

{ id:"mq-806", mod:"M8", topic:"Treatment", diff:3,
  q:"Radiotherapy damages cancer cells more than normal cells mainly because cancer cells",
  options:["divide more frequently, so radiation-induced DNA damage is less often repaired","have thinner cell membranes that allow radiation to enter more easily","contain far more water, which absorbs a greater proportion of the dose","lack mitochondria and therefore cannot repair themselves using ATP"], answer:0,
  why:"Radiation causes DNA damage that is most lethal when a cell attempts to divide before repairing it. Rapidly dividing cells have less time to repair, which is also why hair follicles and gut lining suffer side effects.",
  distractors:{1:"Membrane thickness is not meaningfully different and is not the mechanism.",2:"Water content differences are far too small to explain the selectivity.",3:"Cancer cells retain mitochondria and produce ATP normally."},
  misconception:"The selectivity is statistical, not absolute — which is exactly why side effects appear in other fast-dividing tissues." },

{ id:"mq-807", mod:"M8", topic:"Homeostasis", diff:3,
  q:"Thyroxine secretion is regulated by negative feedback. Rising blood thyroxine causes",
  options:["reduced release of TSH from the pituitary, lowering further secretion","increased release of TSH, so thyroxine output continues to rise","the thyroid gland to enlarge in order to increase its total output","the hypothalamus to raise the body's normal metabolic set point"], answer:0,
  why:"Thyroxine inhibits both the hypothalamus and the anterior pituitary, so TSH falls and the thyroid is stimulated less. The response opposes the change that triggered it, which is what makes it negative feedback.",
  distractors:{1:"That would be positive feedback and would drive the concentration out of control.",2:"Enlargement occurs when TSH stays high, as in iodine deficiency.",3:"The set point is not altered by the hormone it regulates."},
  misconception:"" }

